[Music]  and this is your Tech news briefing for  Wednesday January 18th I'm Zoe Thomas  for The Wall Street Journal  Tick Tock is in the midst of  negotiations with U.S Regulators to keep  the app operating here for the last two  years U.S officials have said they are  worried that tick tock's ownership by  beijing-based bite dance could give  China access to American data or allow  it to influence what U.S users see on  the app and as you may remember that's  led to threats that Tick Tock could be  banned in the U.S to prevent this Tick  Tock is trying a new tactic for  negotiations increased transparency here  with me to discuss what lawmakers and  Regulators are learning about the  company and how it could affect tiktok's  chances of staying in the U.S is our  reporter Georgia Wells hey Georgia  welcome back to the show thank you for  having me so Georgia you've been  speaking with people familiar with these  conversations between Tick Tock and  Regulators what new information is the  company sharing so we're learning a lot  more about what these conversations look  like and what Tick Tock has been doing  for the past two years and so this  involves a you know 1.5 billion dollar  effort so far to set up a new structure  that would be in charge of making sure  that the algorithms involved with the  app and that data it's safe and this is  their attempt to convince the U.S  lawmakers that Tick Tock could operate  in the U.S safely what exactly are they  they proposing to change so Tick Tock  has said that all of the systems it has  related to serving content to users and  determining which videos to take down  that those systems would be housed with  Oracle and the code that runs those  systems would be visible both to Oracle  and to third-party monitors and those  third-party monitors could check the  code to detect whether it had been  manipulated perhaps by the Chinese  government or other foreign actors and  that if they see anything that concerns  them there are stipulations within this  proposal where they would have a way to  escalate their concerns flag them to the  US government and you know ultimately  the US government could shut them down  if it felt that the concerns weren't  taken seriously why has it changed its  approach why is it sharing more now so  typically these conversations are  confidential it's between Tick Tock and  this entity called cypheus which is the  committee on foreign investment in the  U.S and this is an executive branch  panel that kind of looks so whether or  not apps can remain in the US safely and  also related to their ownership and Tick  Tock has been talking to cepheus for two  years  but there's not really a deal in sight  right now so people close to tick tock  are at this point where they want to  show what they've been working on in an  attempt hopefully to find allies in  Washington who could help encourage a  deal to happen we've seen some bands at  state and federal level can you tell us  a bit about those and are they playing a  role in how these conversations are  changing technically that process is  different than this process but yes like  that kind of has this overall ratcheting  up the urgency for tick tock right now  and you've got all of these local  politicians who are talking about Tick  Tock as a risk and so that increases the  urgency for tick tock to want to get a  deal done at the federal level to be  able to show that they're working on  addressing these issues right I mean  lawmakers state and federal and  Regulators have raised these concerns  about tick tocks data being shared with  China or maybe China influencing what  American users see but beyond that  theoretical threat is there any reason  why lawmakers are wary so weeks ago we  learned that Tick Tock conducted this  internal probe to find that employees  had misused their authority to access  the date of journalists on the platform  and this was in an effort to identify  leaks of company information  So that obviously is not doing Tick Tock  any favors when the company is trying to  convince lawmakers that it would never  abuse the data of its users  basically part of the challenge here is  that  tick tock's own Executives in the US  have Not always completely understood  how the algorithms in the app worked and  so back in 2020 there was a group of  tick tock Executives who noticed that  videos for certain creators suddenly  were seeing this big drop in the number  of views they were getting  and they had no idea what had happened  and they like asked around and nobody in  the U.S knows what's going on and then  they talked to colleagues in China and  they learned that there was a tweak that  tick tock's algorithm team in China  implemented changed certain aspects of  content that was shown to users and that  this inadvertently really reduced the  amount of political content that users  were shown and so this kind of showed an  example of take talk could face perhaps  an uphill battle and proving that U.S  Executives would always understand fully  what the app is doing and be in a  position to catch anything that can like  be tweaked with the app and know what's  going on when we asked Tick Tock about  the incident a spokeswoman said that  political content was very popular on  the app in 2020 and she said that the  hashtags Trump Biden Trump 2020 and  Biden 2020 garnered more than 2 trillion  cumulative views monthly at that time  all right I mean those are the sort of  big concerns and as you say they're  certainly not helping Tick Tock in these  negotiations what is Tick Tock saying  that about this kind of change of tactic  of sharing more information with  Regulators in these negotiations so Tick  Tock is at this point where there's not  a deal in sight and so now the company  basically wants to show more of its  homework the company wants to show that  it has been trying to address these  issues that it's working on it so that  in the very least when lawmakers are  talking about the concerns they'll be  armed with more of the facts around what  the company has been doing in an attempt  to address this it sort of feels like  we're hitting this inflection point in a  way lawmakers are paying more attention  to social media the government seems to  have leverage over Tick Tock because of  its ownership but how come other social  media platforms aren't having to offer  up changes to their platforms or reveal  their algorithms to regulators  so these conversations are about  National Security and so that means the  data of the users on the app and also  the content that can be shown to them  and so we are also like you mentioned at  a time when a lot of lawmakers in the US  have concern about the role of tech in  our lives and the role of algorithms and  perhaps like addiction to apps but this  isn't the venue for discussing that like  the venue that we're talking about right  now is just about National Security you  mentioned there's no deal in sight right  now but then what happens next and how  quickly can we expect a resolution to  this issue so that's the big question  potential outcomes could include the US  forcing tick tocks to divest so  basically sell off a portion of its  operations another potential outcome is  there's a bill winding its way through  Congress right now that seems like it  could ban Tick Tock but also we've seen  Tick Tock remain on phones and just have  this issue kind of percolating in the  background for a long time so I don't  know how much longer that could continue  but the million dollar question right  now is what's going to happen to tick  tock in the U.S because this is  something that would affect millions and  millions of users yeah I guess millions  of users will be watching to see if they  can keep that app on their phone Georgia  Wells thank you so much for coming on  the show thank you for having me and  that's it for today's Tech news briefing  for more Tech stories head over to our  website wsj.com I'm Zoe Thomas for The  Wall Street Journal thanks for listening  [Music]  [Music]

